1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:02,999
Hi, we are Team Charlies.

2
00:00:03,149 --> 00:00:07,063
This is Custos: a chain of custody for agent memory.

3
00:00:08,463 --> 00:00:11,727
AI agents now remember things between sessions.

4
00:00:11,877 --> 00:00:14,936
So an attacker does not need to hack the agent.

5
00:00:15,086 --> 00:00:17,380
One false memory is enough.

6
00:00:17,530 --> 00:00:21,654
It acts many sessions later, when no single step looks wrong.

7
00:00:21,804 --> 00:00:27,552
Attacks like MINJA and AgentPoison show this works, and OWASP lists memory poisoning in its

8
00:00:27,552 --> 00:00:28,562
agentic top ten.

9
00:00:30,201 --> 00:00:32,550
Here are two finance agents.

10
00:00:32,700 --> 00:00:37,029
Same Nemotron model, same tools, same inbox.

11
00:00:37,179 --> 00:00:41,702
The only difference: the agent on the right uses memory through Custos.

12
00:00:43,295 --> 00:00:45,949
First, the obvious attack.

13
00:00:46,099 --> 00:00:49,423
An email says Acme's bank account has changed.

14
00:00:49,573 --> 00:00:52,407
The left agent writes it straight into memory.

15
00:00:52,557 --> 00:00:58,171
On the right, Custos stamps the email as T-zero: untrusted outside content.

16
00:00:58,321 --> 00:01:02,435
The claim contradicts the ERP record, which is T-three.

17
00:01:02,585 --> 00:01:06,459
Like in a court, hearsay cannot overrule an official record.

18
00:01:06,609 --> 00:01:08,848
So it goes to quarantine.

19
00:01:10,615 --> 00:01:15,868
Now a smarter attacker: an email about a new payment partner, with a new account.

20
00:01:16,018 --> 00:01:18,353
The left agent stores it again.

21
00:01:18,503 --> 00:01:24,426
On the right, the claim again contradicts the ERP bank account, so Custos holds it.

22
00:01:25,986 --> 00:01:27,551
Days pass.

23
00:01:27,701 --> 00:01:32,029
Sessions four to eight are normal work, and memories get summarised.

24
00:01:33,534 --> 00:01:35,173
Session nine.

25
00:01:35,323 --> 00:01:39,862
The user says: pay Acme's March invoice through the new payment partner.

26
00:01:40,012 --> 00:01:44,551
The left agent pays the attacker: forty lakh rupees, gone.

27
00:01:44,701 --> 00:01:46,990
The right agent pays nobody.

28
00:01:47,140 --> 00:01:52,094
The attacker's account never became trusted memory, and the email waits for a human.

29
00:01:53,661 --> 00:01:56,815
The right agent still tried to act on the email.

30
00:01:56,965 --> 00:02:01,533
It tried four times to send a confirmation, and the gate blocked every one.

31
00:02:01,683 --> 00:02:06,447
Before any high-risk action, Custos checks where each argument came from.

32
00:02:06,597 --> 00:02:11,731
This recipient is the attacker's address, and it traces back to the T-zero email.

33
00:02:11,881 --> 00:02:17,343
Leave-one-out re-planning confirms it: without that email, the agent writes to the ERP

34
00:02:17,343 --> 00:02:18,359
address instead.

35
00:02:19,841 --> 00:02:22,450
Every memory has a chain of custody.

36
00:02:22,600 --> 00:02:28,259
This quarantined claim came from an email, tier T-zero, from an outside domain.

37
00:02:28,409 --> 00:02:33,987
Custos checked it against the ERP record, tier T-three, and found a conflict.

38
00:02:34,137 --> 00:02:36,707
The decision and its rule are on record.

39
00:02:38,636 --> 00:02:44,308
If a reviewer marks the email as poison, one click rolls back the email and everything made

40
00:02:44,308 --> 00:02:45,555
from it, hop by hop.

41
00:02:45,705 --> 00:02:48,434
Then the read log shows who was exposed.

42
00:02:48,584 --> 00:02:53,890
Here, no session ever read the poisoned claim, and the one action that depended on it was

43
00:02:53,890 --> 00:02:54,367
blocked.

44
00:02:54,517 --> 00:02:59,526
The same operation serves a data erasure request under India's DPDP Act.

45
00:03:01,096 --> 00:03:06,524
Every step goes into a signed, hash-chained ledger, rebuilt here from the recorded run.

46
00:03:06,674 --> 00:03:08,809
Verification passes.

47
00:03:08,959 --> 00:03:14,142
Now we edit one entry in a copy, and verification fails at exactly that entry.

48
00:03:15,725 --> 00:03:23,366
On our benchmark, across 7,175 attack episodes, attacks succeeded 27.2 percent of the time

49
00:03:23,366 --> 00:03:27,357
without a defence, and 0.2 percent with Custos.

50
00:03:27,507 --> 00:03:30,681
Two public benchmarks that we did not write agree.

51
00:03:30,831 --> 00:03:36,835
On AgentDojo, injected payments got through 52.1 percent of the time undefended, and never

52
00:03:36,835 --> 00:03:40,838
with Custos asking the user first, at the same task success.

53
00:03:40,988 --> 00:03:46,817
On PoisonedRAG, poisoning against a trusted record fell from 74 to 3 percent.

54
00:03:48,501 --> 00:03:51,860
Built with Nemotron models on NVIDIA GPUs.

55
00:03:52,010 --> 00:03:54,829
Custos: memory is evidence.

56
00:03:54,979 --> 00:03:56,373
Thank you.
